==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|tendernesse}} {{q|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{affix|en|tender|-ness}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɛn.dɚ.nɪs/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɛn.də.nɪs/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tenderness.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ten|der|ness}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# a [[tendency]] to [[express]] [[warm]], [[compassionate]] [[feelings]]
#: {{ux|en|When the lovers were together, their cold indifference gave way to love and '''tenderness'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1853|author=w:Charlotte Brontë|title={{w|Villette (novel)|Villette}}
|passage=I had known him jealous, suspicious; I had seen about him certain '''tendernesses''', fitfulnesses—a softness which came like a warm air, and a ruth which passed like early dew, dried in the heat of his irritabilities: this was all I had seen.}}
# [[concern]] for the feelings or [[welfare]] of others
#: {{ux|en|When they saw the poor orphans, they were overwhelmed with '''tenderness''' for them.}}
#: {{ux|en|Everybody needs a little '''tenderness''' sometimes.}}
# [[pain]] or [[discomfort]] when an affected area is [[touched]]
#: {{ux|en|He noted her extreme '''tenderness''' when he touched the bruise on her thigh.}}

